{
  "about": "Known-answer vectors for pairing a browser extension with the desktop by a one-time code. The code: 16 characters of Crockford's base32 alphabet; the first 15 are the first 75 bits of 10 random bytes read big-endian, 5 bits a character; the 16th is the alphabet's character at the top 5 bits of SHA-256(check_label | the 15 characters). What a person types is read case-insensitively, I and L as 1, O as 0, spaces and hyphens dropped; anything else outside the alphabet is refused. The exchange: th = SHA-256(transcript), transcript = transcript_label(19) | iid(36) | client_public(32) | server_public(32) | client_nonce(16) | server_nonce(16) | port(u32 big-endian) | wallet | major | minor (u16 big-endian each), 161 bytes; shared = X25519, an all-zero result refused; prk = HKDF-SHA-256-Extract(salt = th, ikm = shared | code); k_confirm = HKDF-Expand(prk, confirm_label, 32); secret = HKDF-Expand(prk, secret_label, 32); mac_cli = HMAC-SHA-256(k_confirm, \"cli\" | th); mac_srv = HMAC-SHA-256(k_confirm, \"srv\" | th). Byte strings are lowercase hex.",
  "alphabet": "0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ",
